clove PROTOCOL notes — the I2P BitTorrent dialect and SAM interop
=================================================================

OpenSSH keeps a PROTOCOL file recording where its implementation pins down
things the specs leave vague. This is ours. It doubles as the interop lab
notebook (SCOPE §9): every place the upstream spec is underspecified or a
library/router behaves surprisingly, the observation and clove's decision
land here. Entries are dated by milestone, not calendar.

Status legend:
  [decided]   clove behaves this way; verified in-tree or against a router.
  [assumed]   clove behaves this way; NOT yet verified against a live router.
  [open]      unresolved; needs a router or upstream clarification.


1. Addresses and peer identity
------------------------------

1.1 [decided] Peer identity is the 32-byte SHA-256 of a full I2P
    destination (the "destination hash"). This is the only peer identifier
    the engine handles; there is no IP/port anywhere (SCOPE §3).

1.2 [decided] Outbound dial: a peer known only by its 32-byte hash is dialed
    at "<b32>.b32.i2p", where <b32> is RFC 4648 base32 (lowercase, no
    padding) of the 32 hash bytes. 32 bytes -> 52 base32 chars. The router
    resolves the b32 to a full destination via its netDb.
    Implemented in i2pnet::addr, tested against RFC 4648 vectors.

    1.2a [decided] The decode is strict about the final character's spare
      bits. 52 base32 characters carry 260 bits and a destination hash is
      256, so a lax decoder maps sixteen distinct labels onto one identity
      ("…ljnj" and "…ljna" would name the same peer). Found by the mutation
      sweep in i2pnet::addr, and refused: a decoder must not accept what its
      own encoder cannot produce, the same rule as a resume bitfield's
      trailing bits. No interop cost — every implementation derives the label
      as base32(SHA-256(dest)), which always leaves those bits zero — so a
      label with them set was not produced by any conforming client.

1.3 [decided] Inbound / naming: SAM reports a peer's *full* base64
    destination (I2P's base64 alphabet is standard base64 with '+'->'-' and
    '/'->'~'). Its hash is SHA-256 of the decoded destination bytes. These
    compose: base32(SHA-256(dest)) is exactly the b32 label, so a dialed
    peer and an accepted peer reconcile to the same DestHash.

1.4 [decided] Compact tracker peer lists on I2P are concatenated 32-byte
    destination hashes, no port (unlike clearnet's 6-byte ip:port). Non-
    compact responses give full base64 destinations under each peer dict's
    `ip` key. Both parsed in clove-core::tracker.


5. HTTP tracker announces (clove-core::tracker, M3)
---------------------------------------------------

5.1 [assumed] An announce is an HTTP/1.1 GET to the tracker's I2P
    destination. clove sends the standard BEP 3 query params plus:
      - `compact=1` (we require the 32-byte-hash compact form, §1.4);
      - `port=6881` as a placeholder — I2P has no port, but trackers/params
        parsers expect the key;
      - `ip=<our base64 destination>` — the I2P convention for telling the
        tracker our destination, since there is no IP to observe. This is
        what i2psnark does; verify the exact value form (bare base64 vs.
        with a suffix) against a live postman/opentracker announce. [open]

5.2 [decided] Any query already present in the announce URL (e.g. postman's
    `/announce.php?...`) is preserved; clove appends its params with the
    correct `?`/`&` separator.

5.3 [decided] Announce timing: honor the tracker's `interval`, floored at a
    local 60s minimum so a misconfigured tracker cannot make us hammer.
    On failure, exponential backoff from 30s capped at 30 min. A tracker
    `failure reason` is surfaced to the operator, not retried as if
    transient.

5.4 [open] Whether i2psnark/opentracker expect `event=started` on the very
    first announce only, and any tracker-specific quirks (numwant caps,
    `&supportcrypto`, etc.), to be confirmed live at M3 and recorded here.


2. SAM / yosemite behavior (from the 0.7.0 API review, M0/M1)
------------------------------------------------------------

2.1 [decided] yosemite hardcodes the SAM host to 127.0.0.1; only the TCP
    port is configurable. This happens to enforce Layer 1's loopback-only
    rule for free. Consequence: the documented `--i-know-sam-is-remote`
    escape hatch (SCOPE §5) is NOT expressible through the yosemite backend
    as it stands — a remote SAM bridge would need a different backend or an
    upstream change. Noted rather than worked around.

2.2 [decided] yosemite's synchronous Stream exposes `split(self) ->
    (ReadHalf, WriteHalf)`, not a duplicating `try_clone`. clove's
    `I2pStream` trait was corrected to a split-based shape (Phase D) so the
    mock and the SAM backend share one honest abstraction, and it matches
    the reader-thread/writer-thread model (Q5) directly.

2.3 [assumed] yosemite's `Session::connect` (sync) has no timeout parameter
    and blocks until the STREAM CONNECT resolves. clove's dial() takes a
    timeout for the trait's sake but cannot enforce it at this layer; the
    timeout is a supervision-layer concern. Whether long connects need a
    watchdog is an [open] R2 question for the stress harness.

2.4 [assumed] `Session::connect`/`accept`/`forward` all take `&mut self` and
    block. A single Session shared between the dial path and an accept path
    therefore serializes. clove's SAM backend currently implements only the
    outbound + naming paths on the shared Session (behind a Mutex); the
    inbound topology is deferred (see 2.5).

2.5 [assumed] Inbound streams use STREAM FORWARD, not STREAM ACCEPT. ACCEPT
    is one accept per inbound stream on &mut self and serializes every
    inbound peer on the one session; FORWARD has the router push each inbound
    stream as a fresh TCP connection to a loopback listener we own (an
    allowed Layer-1 IP socket), so inbound concurrency is a plain accept loop,
    not a lock. This matches the "one PRIMARY session + forwarded listener"
    topology in SCOPE §4. Implemented in i2pnet::sam (SamListener /
    ForwardedStream), decision recorded in LIVE-TESTING.md §3.
    With SILENT=false (yosemite's default, set explicitly) the router prepends
    each forwarded connection with the peer's base64 destination line,
    optionally followed by FROM_PORT/TO_PORT params, terminated by '\n'; we
    read up to that newline one byte at a time (so the peer's stream payload
    is left untouched) and derive the DestHash via §1.3. The split/parse logic
    is unit-tested over loopback TCP; the exact forwarded framing and that a
    forwarded peer's derived hash reconciles with its dialed hash are the
    first things confirmed against a live router (LIVE-TESTING.md §6.1, M1).

2.6 [open] R2 — i2pd SAM behavior under many concurrent streams on one
    session is the suspected root of XD-style flakiness. The reconnect
    supervisor (i2pnet::supervisor) is built and unit-tested against a fake
    factory; its behavior against a *real* router mid-restart, and the
    concurrency ceiling of one session, are the harness's job. First live
    observations against i2pd (M1, in progress):

    2.6a [decided] Outbound dial *initiation* serializes per session. yosemite's
      sync `connect` takes `&mut self` and blocks until the router resolves the
      stream, so SamSession::dial holds the session mutex for the whole connect.
      Established streams run concurrently (each is an independent socket once
      connected); only the setup step is serial. Consequence for the engine:
      one dial session initiates peers one-at-a-time. Whether that needs
      multiple dial sessions, or is fine because connects are quick once warm,
      is the N-scaling question the harness still has to answer.

    2.6b [assumed] A freshly created transient destination is briefly
      unreachable: a dial to it returns STREAM STATUS RESULT=CANT_REACH_PEER
      (surfaced as Protocol(Router(CantReachPeer))) for the ~seconds it takes
      the destination to build tunnels and publish its leaseSet. This is normal
      warmup, not a fault; the live test and sam-stress retry the dial through
      it, as the real peer-acquisition path must. Distinct from a *cold router*
      (reseed incomplete → no peers → CantReachPeer indefinitely), which is an
      operator/setup problem (see LIVE-TESTING.md troubleshooting).

2.7 [decided] yosemite 0.7.0 sets no read or write timeout on its SAM control
    socket. Against a bridge that accepts the TCP connection and then says
    nothing, `Session::new` blocks forever — it does not error, so nothing
    upstream of it can back off, retry, or log. Measured in-tree against a
    fake bridge: of six misbehaviours (close, silence, garbage, endless
    bytes, refuse-hello, one-byte-dribble), four hung indefinitely and only
    the two that closed or answered returned at all.

    clove's answer is a pre-flight probe (i2pnet::sam::probe_bridge): before
    yosemite is handed the port, clove opens its own socket with a connect
    timeout, a per-read timeout, a whole-exchange deadline and a 512-byte cap,
    speaks `HELLO VERSION` itself, and requires `HELLO REPLY RESULT=OK`. All
    six cases now fail cleanly and quickly, with an error naming the address.
    Budget is SamConfig::probe_timeout (default 10s).

    Residual gap, deliberately not papered over: a bridge that answers HELLO
    correctly and *then* stalls on SESSION CREATE still hangs inside
    yosemite. The probe cannot see that far. Closing it needs either read
    timeouts upstream in yosemite, or proxying yosemite's control connection
    through a socket clove owns so it can be shut down — the latter is real
    complexity for a failure mode much rarer than "the router is not up yet",
    so it waits for evidence from live running. Tracked by the test
    `a_bridge_that_passes_hello_and_then_stalls_is_a_known_gap`.

2.8 [open] emissary 0.4.0, first live run (2026-07-25): every dial between two
    destinations on the *same* emissary router failed. The router's own log is
    unambiguous about why:

      WARN emissary::sam::session: unable to open stream, lease set not found
           session_id="clove-stress-dial" destination_id=uLFobsL8 error=Timeout

    Not CANT_REACH_PEER from a failed tunnel build: the dialing session cannot
    resolve the *leaseSet* of a destination hosted by the very router it is
    talking to. The target session's tunnels were up at the time — the same log
    shows "inbound tunnel built name=clove-stress-listen" — so this is a netDb
    lookup that goes to the network and times out, rather than being answered
    locally. 19 such timeouts across the run; zero dials succeeded.

    Also seen, immediately after each of that session's inbound tunnel builds:

      WARN emissary::tunnel::pool: unhandled message name=clove-stress-listen
           message_type=DeliveryStatus

    DeliveryStatus is how a netDb store is acknowledged, so an unhandled one is
    consistent with the leaseSet never being recorded as published.

    Two candidate explanations, not yet separated:
      (a) emissary does not short-circuit leaseSet lookups for destinations it
          hosts itself, so a same-router dial needs a full floodfill round trip
          — which a young router with a thin netDb cannot complete. Java I2P
          and i2pd both answer locally, which is why the loopback topology
          (LIVE-TESTING §5.2) was assumed cheap.
      (b) the router was simply too young (~30 min, first boot) and would
          succeed given hours.

    Distinguishing them is one run: leave the router up for several hours and
    repeat `make router-ready ROUTER=emissary`. Still failing ⇒ (a), and an
    upstream report with this log. Succeeding ⇒ (b), and LIVE-TESTING gains a
    minimum-router-age note.

    Nothing here implicates clove: the dial is an ordinary SAM STREAM CONNECT
    to a b32, and the router refuses it before any of our code is involved. It
    is recorded because SCOPE §6 predicted exactly this ("young router, young
    SAM impl — expect to find bugs on both sides; coordinate upstream"), and
    because the same *symptom* (CantReachPeer) appeared earlier against i2pd
    without a log detailed enough to say whether the cause was the same.


3. Reconnect discipline (i2pnet::supervisor, M1)
------------------------------------------------

3.1 [decided] On session loss the whole session tree is rebuilt on an
    exponential backoff (default 1s doubling to 60s) with jitter (default
    0.2) to avoid a thundering-herd reconnect across many torrents. Torrents
    see a visible "waiting for router" state, never a hard failure.

3.2 [decided] A healthy session that dies once retries promptly: only failed
    *rebuilds* grow the backoff counter, not the loss itself. This keeps a
    one-off router blip from imposing a long stall.


4. Wire protocol (clove-core::wire, M2)
---------------------------------------

4.1 [decided] The peer wire protocol above the I2P stream is byte-identical
    to clearnet BEP 3 — no ports appear in any message; the peer is an I2P
    destination at the layer below. BEP 6 (fast) and BEP 10 (extended) are
    fully framed by the codec.

4.2 [decided] Block size is fixed at 16 KiB (BLOCK_LEN); requests and piece
    blocks over that are rejected. Matches i2psnark.

4.3 [decided] i2p_pex (clove-core::pex): a bencoded dict with `added` and
    `dropped` byte strings, each concatenated 32-byte destination hashes.
    Unlike clearnet ut_pex there are no `added.f` flag bytes and no
    IPv6/`added6` keys; any extra keys a peer sends are ignored. A message
    over MAX_PEX_PEERS (512) is rejected as spam. [open] whether i2psnark
    ever attaches flags we should honor — confirm live, treat i2psnark as
    normative (R4).

4.4 [decided] ut_metadata (BEP 9, clove-core::metadata): standard 16 KiB
    metadata pieces; a data message is a bencoded {msg_type,piece,
    total_size} header immediately followed by the raw piece bytes.
    Reassembled bytes are SHA-1-verified against the info-hash before use,
    so a lying peer cannot substitute a different torrent. `total_size`
    over 8 MiB is refused up front. Extension ids: clove advertises
    i2p_pex=1, ut_metadata=2 and replies to a peer using the ids that peer
    advertised (peers reply to us using ours). fetch_metadata drives the
    magnet bootstrap: BT+extension handshake, request all pieces, verify,
    then MetaInfo::from_info_dict. Verified in-tree over the mock (a client
    fetches an info dict from a serving instance).

4.5 [decided] Magnet links (clove-core::magnet): `magnet:?xt=urn:btih:<hex
    40 | base32 32>`; `dn` and `tr` are percent-decoded, `tr` filtered to
    I2P-only like .torrent announce URLs.

4.6 [open] I2P `maggot://` links are a distinct, underspecified format.
    Not parsed yet — we will not guess a grammar we cannot verify against
    real examples. Add support (and the grammar) here once confirmed.
